.TH TKNODE 2
.SH NAME
tknode \- decode libtk's render-node tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "draw.m";
include "tknode.m";
tknode := load Tknode Tknode->PATH;

Tgroup, Tcolour, Troundfill, Tborder, Tcorners,
Tlayer, Tlayerend, Ttexture, Tglyphs, Tlinear,
Tshadow, Tclip, Ttransform, Topacity, Tellipse,
Tellipsefill, Tpolyfill, Tspline, Tclipend,
Ttransformend, Topacityend: con iota;
Nodetypes: con Topacityend+1;

Node: adt {
    ntype:    int;
    r:        Draw\->Rect;
    dstr:     Draw\->Rect;
    sp:       Draw\->Point;
    rgba:     int;
    radius:   int;
    width:    int;
    pts:      array of Draw\->Point;
    fontname: string;
    text:     string;

    name: fn(n: self ref Node): string;
};

init:     fn();
decode:   fn(dump: string): (array of ref Node, string);
typename: fn(t: int): string;
typeof:   fn(name: string): int;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libtk
can describe a widget as a tree of render nodes instead of painting
straight into an image, and can serialise the tree it rendered.
.B Tknode
decodes that serialisation, so a consumer \(em recording, sending a
window somewhere else to draw, a backend that wants geometry rather
than a raster \(em can be written outside
.IR libtk .
.PP
.I Init
must be called before
.IR decode ,
which loads
.B Sys
and
.B Draw
itself if it was not.
.PP
A node holds
.B no display\-side resource:
a colour is an
.B rgba
number rather than an allocated
.BR Image ,
and a face or a bitmap is a file
.B name
rather than a loaded
.BR Font .
That is what makes a tree writable down at all, and it is why a decoded
.B Node
is useful to a program that has no
.B Display
of its own.
.SH "GETTING A TREE"
Recording is off by default, because it costs a serialisation on the
path every repaint takes:
.IP
.EX
tk\->cmd(top, "render record 1");
\&...                   # whatever repaints
dump := tk\->cmd(top, "render tree");
(nodes, err) := tknode\->decode(dump);
.EE
.PP
.B "render tree"
answers the last tree the walk rendered, and the empty string when
nothing has been recorded.
.B "render record 0"
stops recording and drops the tree, so an answer after it is empty
rather than stale.
.SH "THE WIRE FORM"
One node per line:
.IP
.EX
NAME r.min.x r.min.y r.max.x r.max.y \e
     dstr.min.x dstr.min.y dstr.max.x dstr.max.y \e
     sp.x sp.y rgba radius width npts [x y]... \e
     "fontname" "text"
.EE
.PP
all on one line.
.B NAME
is the type's name, not its number, so a dump is readable and survives
being looked at; the number is in
.B ntype
after decoding and is the enum's, which does not move.
.PP
The two strings are
.B quoted and escaped
\(em backslash, quote, newline and tab \(em because both of them can
hold anything a program put there. A canvas text item's string can
contain a newline, and an unquoted rest\-of\-line field would decode
that as the start of another node.
.SH "FIELDS THAT ARE NOT WHAT THEY LOOK LIKE"
Three node types use the fields differently and a consumer has to know:
.TP
.B Tellipse, Tellipsefill
.B r.min
is the
.B centre
and
.B sp
is the two
.BR radii ,
because an ellipse is a centre and two radii and a bounding rectangle
loses the distinction between a radius of
.I r
and a box
.I 2r+1
wide.
.B r.max
is a copy of
.BR r.min .
.TP
.B Tspline
.B sp
is
.B not a point.
It carries the two end styles, first and last, packed \(em a canvas
line's arrow is 25329922, which is why they are here and not in
.BR radius ,
which a
.B short
would truncate.
.TP
.B Tlayer
.B r.max
is the scratch surface's
.BR extent ,
.B dstr
is where the result lands and
.B sp
is the source point of the blit.
.PP
.B Ttexture 's
.B width
says how the image is used: 1 for a
.BR mask ,
with
.B rgba
the colour painted through it, and 0 for a source drawn as it stands.
.SH "SPANS"
.BR Tclip ,
.B Ttransform
and
.B Topacity
each open a span that the matching
.BR Tclipend ,
.B Ttransformend
or
.B Topacityend
closes, because each is a property of a
.B run
of nodes rather than of one and a flat list has nowhere else to put it.
A consumer walks the list in order and maintains its own stack; the
ends are at the end of the enum rather than beside their openers
because a node type's number must not move once a backend has seen one.
.PP
.B Topacity 's
alpha is the low byte of its
.BR rgba .
.B Ttransform
carries its translation in
.B r.min
and a uniform scale in
.BR sp :
.B sp.x
over
.BR sp.y ,
a rational because the renderer has no floating point. There is no
rotation, deliberately \(em a rotation maps a rectangle to something
that is not one.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/tknode.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tk (2),
.IR tkscenetest (1),
.B doc/scene\-graph\-plan.ms
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Decode
returns the nodes it managed and a non\-nil error describing the first
line it could not read, rather than nothing: a truncated tree is more
use than an empty one when the question is what went wrong.
.PP
.I Typeof
returns \-1 for a name it does not know, and
.I Node.name
answers
.B unknown
for a number it does not know. That is what a tree from a newer
.I libtk
looks like, and a consumer should treat an unknown node as something to
skip rather than as a corrupt dump.
.SH BUGS
It does not cover
.B Tgroup ,
.B Tlinear
or
.B Tshadow ,
which are declared in the vocabulary and emitted by nothing, so no
dump has ever contained one.
.PP
A dump is the tree
.B after
the walk rendered it, which means after any global scale was applied to
the nodes \(em so a dump taken under
.B "render scaled 2"
holds the scaled geometry, not the original. The node transform is
different: a
.B Ttransform
node is in the tree and its span is
.B not
pre\-scaled, so a consumer applies it itself.
.PP
There is no encoder. A dump can be read but not written back, so
nothing yet proves a decoded tree is
.I sufficient
to reproduce the picture \(em only that every field survives the trip.
Reproducing it would mean a consumer that rasterises the primitive
subset, which is the next thing here rather than a thing in the way:
every renderer case is built from
.IR draw (2),
.IR ellipse (2),
.IR fillpoly (2)
and
.IR bezspline (2),
and the three composite kinds
.BR ( Troundfill ,
.BR Tcorners ,
.BR Tborder )
are solid rectangles and a per\-row corner cut, so none of it needs
anything a Limbo program cannot reach.
